= Sun Yat-sen's "Northern Expedition"

An ailment has affected our dear leader's health. Many in the party have told him to rest and seek treatment, but Sun decided to travel north to do some final acts.
In Tianjin, He called for the end of the warlords and the abolition of the unequal treaties with the Imperialist West. He delivered a speech to suggest a gathering of a "national conference" for the Chinese people. All things that he believed most and what he hoped would stoke the flames of revolution back into the Chinese people, as it was before in 1911.

He went to Japan and spoke about Pan-Asianism, because of the Japanese's help to him in exile when he was still in the China Revival Society days.
Finally, he went to Beijing to discuss the future of the country, the country he had failed to turn to democracy but still hoped would return to it.

Next month, Sun would stay in Beijing to undergo a surgery to figure out what was happening to him. 
The party is now in trepidation. Sun Yat-sen's death could very well mean a power struggle. But, he held out for hope.

Hope, that his Three Principles survive through the storm.
